  "mainEntity": {
    "name": "Repairing vs Replacing a Sticking Kitchen Door Frame: How to Decide",
    "text": "Repairing vs Replacing a Sticking Kitchen Door Frame: How to Decide",
    "@type": "Question",
    "answerCount": 1,
    "acceptedAnswer": {
      "text": "When a kitchen door starts sticking or rubbing against the frame, homeowners often wonder if they can fix the existing jamb or if they need an entirely new pre-hung unit.\n\nWhat Can Change the Right Choice\nIn most kitchen settings, whether you repair or replace depends on jamb alignment, moisture exposure, and structural integrity. If the problem stems from loose hinge screws, minor settling, or slightly shifted shims, a contractor can easily re-square the opening and tighten the hardware. You can check this by measuring the jamb opening diagonally from corner to corner. If those two measurements match closely and the wood remains firm, repairing the frame is usually fast, cost-effective, and avoids disturbing nearby kitchen tile, drywall, or paint.\n\nHowever, if diagonal measurements show substantial racking, or if water near the floor has caused the lower jamb to turn soft and spongy, a localized splice or a full frame replacement is required. Painting over rotted wood will not hold hinge screws, and adjusting hinges cannot compensate for a severely twisted frame. \n\nIf your kitchen door jamb is binding, crumbling at the base, or showing sheetrock cracks around the casing, professional assessment keeps the repair clean. Abraham Becerra at HH Remodeling LLC handles door frame repairs, custom framing, and drywall repair across the Denver area. Call 303-505-0866 to schedule an inspection.",
      "@type": "Answer",
      "author": {
        "name": "HH Remodeling LLC",
        "@type": "Organization"
      }
    }
  }
}